  "title": {
    "en": "Mixed plumages: finches, shrikes and waxwings"
  },
  "subtitle": {
    "en": "Males, females and youngsters that look like different birds"
  },
  "intro": {
    "en": "The same species can look like three different birds. A male Crossbill is red, a female is green, a young bird is streaky. A male Red-backed Shrike has a grey head and a chestnut back, a female is brown with a scaly belly. This lesson is the gauntlet: it pushes you to name each bird in a plumage that you do not expect.\n\nThe birds are the winter finches and the shrikes. Bramblings, Twites and Redpolls arrive from Scandinavia in autumn and winter, in numbers that vary from year to year. Crossbills, Hawfinches and Waxwings can come in irruptions, when a food shortage in the north sends them south. The two shrikes are quite different: the Great Grey Shrike is a winter visitor, the Red-backed a summer one.\n\nThe plumages we use here are chosen for their difficulty: the female Crossbill and the female Red-backed Shrike are harder than the males. In the quiz you may meet the same species in a different plumage from the one you learned."
  },
  "words": [
    {
      "term": {
        "en": "irruption"
      },
      "explain": {
        "en": "A sudden, large movement of a species outside its normal range, driven by a food shortage in the north."
      }
    },
    {
      "term": {
        "en": "mask"
      },
      "explain": {
        "en": "A dark patch through the eye. Shrikes have one; its size and shape differ."
      }
    },
    {
      "term": {
        "en": "crossed bill"
      },
      "explain": {
        "en": "A bill with tips that cross over, which a Crossbill uses to open pine cones."
      }
    },
    {
      "term": {
        "en": "rump"
      },
      "explain": {
        "en": "The lower back, above the tail. White in a Brambling, pink in a male Twite."
      }
    }
  ],
